## Where this stops

The address cannot be derived, and that ends the setup before the summary table. Neither rule fires: the folder is not a git working copy (no `.git` anywhere up to the mount point, so no remote), and it is not inside a synced store (`tmpfs` on `/tmp`). Per decision 0023, a project scope's address is required — `0007`'s fourth form, `Address: none`, is for component blocks only, and a scope adopted with it hands every future component a stub address that cannot pass the checks.

So, in the framework's words:

> This folder has no address yet, and a project needs one before I can set it up. Every folder you later add to this project keeps its own copy of this address, and that copy is how anyone working in one of those folders finds their way back here. So it has to be something that works from a different machine, rather than a path on this one.
>
> Two things count. A git remote you have pushed to, which gives an address like `https://github.com/you/northwind`. Or a folder inside a synced store — OneDrive, Google Drive, Dropbox — which gives one like `OneDrive, Projects/pinewood-ceramics`.
>
> `git init` on its own does not count. It gives this folder a history and leaves it in exactly one place, and what is being asked for is a second place.
>
> Set one of those up, or move the folder into one, and tell me when it is done. I will read the address from the folder rather than from your description. Until then there is nothing I can write, so setup stops here.

**No files written or changed.** Your four answers are recorded above; when the folder has an address, setup resumes at the summary table and does not need the interview again.
